{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AQpxmgxRDQr8WDypnoy6ve","type":"tool_result","content":"### Work one example, once\n\nTen replicas. Both fields left at their defaults.\n\n```\n  maxSurge        = 25% of 10 = 2.5  →  round UP   →  3\n  maxUnavailable  = 25% of 10 = 2.5  →  round DOWN →  2\n\n  ceiling on total Pods      = 10 + 3 = 13\n=== Succeeded/Complete in §7 ===\n698:Chapter 5 taught you five Pod phases and gave you an immediate use for three of them. Here are the other two. `Succeeded` means all containers in the Pod terminated in success and will not be restarted; `Failed` means all containers terminated and at least one terminated in failure [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. For everything in §1 through §6, those two phases were terminal states you hoped never to see. For a Job, `Succeeded` is the *point*. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — the five Pod phases]*\n1118:B) `Succeeded` — all containers terminated in success and will not be restarted\n1119:C) `Completed` — a Job-specific phase not available to other Pods\n1183:**Q16 — B.** `Succeeded` means all containers in the Pod have terminated in success and will not be restarted [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. **A** confuses in-progress with done. **C** invents a phase; there are five, and `Completed` is not among them [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23], though you will see the word in `kubectl get pods` output as a container-state reason, which is exactly why the distractor works. **D** is the failure phase: all containers terminated and at least one terminated in failure [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23].","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49650605-8009-40d5-9276-2f4d91700b57","uuid":"e7ff431e-075c-4a81-ad56-10eeec1ef38f","timestamp":"2026-08-24T15:38:24.386Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### Work one example, once\n\nTen replicas. Both fields left at their defaults.\n\n```\n  maxSurge        = 25% of 10 = 2.5  →  round UP   →  3\n  maxUnavailable  = 25% of 10 = 2.5  →  round DOWN →  2\n\n  ceiling on total Pods      = 10 + 3 = 13\n=== Succeeded/Complete in §7 ===\n698:Chapter 5 taught you five Pod phases and gave you an immediate use for three of them. Here are the other two. `Succeeded` means all containers in the Pod terminated in success and will not be restarted; `Failed` means all containers terminated and at least one terminated in failure [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. For everything in §1 through §6, those two phases were terminal states you hoped never to see. For a Job, `Succeeded` is the *point*. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — the five Pod phases]*\n1118:B) `Succeeded` — all containers terminated in success and will not be restarted\n1119:C) `Completed` — a Job-specific phase not available to other Pods\n1183:**Q16 — B.** `Succeeded` means all containers in the Pod have terminated in success and will not be restarted [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. **A** confuses in-progress with done. **C** invents a phase; there are five, and `Completed` is not among them [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23], though you will see the word in `kubectl get pods` output as a container-state reason, which is exactly why the distractor works. **D** is the failure phase: all containers terminated and at least one terminated in failure [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
