{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qdq9tLJGHG3yUwXe376KMS","type":"tool_result","content":"165:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n231:$ kubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=5\n239:[Omitted long matching line]\n268:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n429:The other strategy is the contrast that makes the first one legible. With `Recreate`, all existing Pods are killed before new ones are created [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n451:[Omitted long matching line]\n479:A Deployment's revision is created when a rollout is triggered, and **a new revision is created if and only if the Deployment's Pod template (`.spec.template`) is changed.** Other updates, such as scaling the Deployment, do not create a revision [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n483:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n527:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n644:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n680:A **DaemonSet** ensures that all (or some) nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to the cluster, Pods are added to them; as nodes are removed, those Pods are garbage collected. Deleting the DaemonSet cleans up the Pods it created [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n704:A **CronJob** creates Jobs on a repeating schedule, and one CronJob object is like one line of a crontab file on a Unix system [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24]. It is meant for regular scheduled actions such as backups and report generation [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24].\n739:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n745:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n779:The **CustomResourceDefinition**, the CRD, is the API resource that lets you define custom resources. Defining a CRD object creates a new custom resource with a name and schema that you specify, and the Kubernetes API then serves and handles the storage of it for you. This frees you from writing your own API server, though the generic nature of the implementation means you have less flexibility than with API-server aggregation [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n789:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n803:The **operator pattern** combines custom resources and custom controllers [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n929:> ☀️ **Zenith:** Six resources. One shape. You have been looking at the same diagram for the entire chapter."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49650605-8009-40d5-9276-2f4d91700b57","uuid":"7c58c697-adb0-4347-9731-d446b8392e2e","timestamp":"2026-08-24T15:40:31.788Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"165:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n231:$ kubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=5\n239:[Omitted long matching line]\n268:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n429:The other strategy is the contrast that makes the first one legible. With `Recreate`, all existing Pods are killed before new ones are created [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n451:[Omitted long matching line]\n479:A Deployment's revision is created when a rollout is triggered, and **a new revision is created if and only if the Deployment's Pod template (`.spec.template`) is changed.** Other updates, such as scaling the Deployment, do not create a revision [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n483:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n527:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n644:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n680:A **DaemonSet** ensures that all (or some) nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to the cluster, Pods are added to them; as nodes are removed, those Pods are garbage collected. Deleting the DaemonSet cleans up the Pods it created [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n704:A **CronJob** creates Jobs on a repeating schedule, and one CronJob object is like one line of a crontab file on a Unix system [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24]. It is meant for regular scheduled actions such as backups and report generation [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24].\n739:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n745:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n779:The **CustomResourceDefinition**, the CRD, is the API resource that lets you define custom resources. Defining a CRD object creates a new custom resource with a name and schema that you specify, and the Kubernetes API then serves and handles the storage of it for you. This frees you from writing your own API server, though the generic nature of the implementation means you have less flexibility than with API-server aggregation [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n789:> ★ **Fixed Point:**\n803:The **operator pattern** combines custom resources and custom controllers [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n929:> ☀️ **Zenith:** Six resources. One shape. You have been looking at the same diagram for the entire chapter.","numLines":18,"totalLines":18}}
